attempt releases its slot after owned executor cleanup, before waiting in the separate
arrival-ordered integration queue. `runIssue` remains the sole retry loop. All public
integration calls share that queue, including single-issue calls. Submitted work never
unlocks dependencies; integrated work with omissions does, carrying verification debt.
Exhaustion blocks descendants without terminating independent branches. Repeated
scheduler calls share the canonical repository/run owner rather than redispatching.
The existing active-run repository lock remains separate from worker allocation.

Executor worktrees refresh the integration base at dispatch preparation; each candidate
refreshes it when serial integration starts. The existing exact-commit validation and
expected-old ref advance remain authoritative. Concurrent journal writes now retry
revision conflicts while checking attempt/approval/control identity. Timeout omission
persistence similarly retries unrelated journal revisions without inventing a repair.
Durable `execution.observed` / `issue.changed` events and `/ralph-status` show readiness,
queue order, blockers, checks and qualified verification.

Approved command configuration now supports `resources` (environment source name to
port/namespace/directory allocation) and `exclusiveResources` for shared resources.
Discovery proposes these through `/ralph-commands`; approval/hash/verification still
cover the exact configuration. Commands reference allocation through existing env
mappings; no commands are invented or rewritten. Executors prepare approved lifecycle
commands before dispatch, using main-session permission while their executor session
is not yet bound. Their prompts/attempt evidence retain allocation and lifecycle results.
Candidates receive fresh environments. Startup without allocated ports serializes use;
explicit shared resource names serialize their use as well. Allocation and directory
cleanup record intent before effects. Idempotent ownership-based cleanup releases only
the job'"'s ports/directories/locks after process cleanup succeeds, retaining failures.
Missing references/capabilities are setup blockers; denial/cancellation wait.

